{"name":"Pomelo Sugar","hex":"#fce8e3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pomelo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The large fruit of the Citrus maxima (syn. C. grandis), native to South Asia and Southeast Asia, with a thick green or yellow rind, a thick white pith, and semi-sweet translucent pale flesh.","The tree which produces this fruit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pomelo"},{"term":"sugar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A food consisting of small, sweet crystals, principally of sucrose, obtained from sugar cane or sugar beet and used as sweetener and preservative.","Any specific variety of sugar."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add sugar to; to sweeten with sugar.","To make (something unpleasant) seem less so."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sugar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pomelo","blocks":[{"text":"The pomelo, also known as a shaddock, is the largest citrus fruit. It is an ancestor of several cultivated citrus species, including the bitter orange and the grapefruit. It is a natural, non-hybrid citrus fruit, native to Southeast Asia. Similar in taste to a sweet grapefruit, the pomelo is commonly eaten and used for festive occasions throughout Southeast and East Asia. As with the grapefruit, phytochemicals in the pomelo have the potential for drug interactions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Pomelo_fruit.jpg/330px-Pomelo_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomelo","wikidata":"Q80024"},{"title":"Sugar","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar is a class of sweet-tasting, soluble carbohydrates, many of which are used in food. Simple sugars, also called monosaccharides, include glucose, fructose and galactose. Compound sugars, also called disaccharides or double sugars, are molecules made of two bonded monosaccharides; common examples are sucrose, lactose and maltose. White sugar is almost pure sucrose. During digestion, compound sugars are hydrolysed into simple sugars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg/330px-Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar","wikidata":"Q11002"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Pomelo_seedling.jpg/250px-Pomelo_seedling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Pomelo_seedling.jpg/330px-Pomelo_seedling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Pomelo_seedling.jpg/250px-Pomelo_seedling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APomelo_seedling.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Pomelo_flower.jpg/250px-Pomelo_flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Pomelo_flower.jpg/330px-Pomelo_flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Pomelo_flower.jpg/250px-Pomelo_flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APomelo_flower.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/B%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Fi.jpg/250px-B%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Fi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/B%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Fi.jpg/500px-B%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Fi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thum